===Golden Skin Strategies===
*'''For the first quest''', try going towards Floor 20+, since Twisted Gourdy is guaranteed to spawn every [[Floor]] that is a multiple of 20, not to mention Twisted Gourdy being able to spawn before Floor 20 too. You can play with a distractor such as {{CI|Looey|name=true}} or {{CI|Eclipse|name=true}} to make the progression easier and to let them bring Twisted Gourdy to the Elevator for the encounter, or you can choose to solo.
*'''For the second quest''', playing in a team can be good as both of Gourdy's Abilities are primarily suited for selflessness, and can make things more efficient. {{TI|Memory Locket|name=true}} will always highlight healing Items when you're on one Heart, so take advantage of its mechanic to ensure you don't die before completing Floor 20.
*'''For the third quest''', just try to open at least one Trick or Treat Door every Floor and you should be able to complete this quest fairly quickly. You may instead find it easier to play in a solo run so you can open every Trick or Treat Door that spawns.

==Strategies==
With max extraction thanks to 5 {{Star}} in both Skill Check and Extraction Speed (tied with {{CI|Soulvester|name=true}}), alongside 4 Star [[Movement Speed]] and 1 Star [[Stealth]], Gourdy is suitable for almost any role, being an amazing and extremely versatile all-rounder. His only genuine con is his 1 Star Stamina (and his 1 Star Stealth if you intend to play him as an extractor, since you could interfere with your distractor often), but this can be easily mitigated with {{TI|Cooler|name=true}} or simply good Stamina management, especially if you have played [[Toons]] with similar Stats, and the 1 Star Stealth can be a non-factor by being aware of how to manage [[Twisteds]].

Gourdy's Active [[Ability]], {{AI|Gourdy|name=true}}, functions similarly to {{CI|Toodles|name=true}}' Active Ability, however it is slightly more potent (+5% extra compared to {{AI|Toodles}}'''Beginner's Luck''') and gives the boost to surrounding teammates within a small radius instead of boosting himself, in addition to it having an extra 10 seconds of cooldown. Unlike most random-effect boosts, it will actually show what the effect is (at least on your target's screen) making it easier to figure out what to do with the buff. His Ability's randomness means it shouldn't be fully relied on to help teammates (ex. giving Skill Check to a Toon being chased), but it is still a helpful tool if you roll something good for your team. Also note his radius is quite small, so you have to practically hug your target, but his 1 Star Stealth can help if you want to boost a teammate who is being chased by taking aggro away from them. Try not to boost distractors (unless in [[Panic Mode]]!); you'll take aggro away from them by accident unless your distractor is playing {{CI|Pebble|name=true}} and {{AI|Pebble}}'''Speak!''' is active, they use an {{II|Air Horn|name=true}}, or they're playing as a 1 Star Stealth Toon (which you may still take aggro from if you get too close, due to percentage based Stealth decreasing Trinkets like {{TI|Scrapbook|name=true}} not affecting them). His Ability could be used through a wall or obstacle near the distractor, but due to the boost that he gives being randomized, it isn't worthwhile unless they're struggling. Ask teammates to group around you before the [[Elevator]] opens to boost a lot of players at once. Try to use '''Trick or Treat''' on extracting Toons as you pass them—your 1 Star Stealth makes it easy even while distracting as long as you understand line of sight. You can also ask teammates to run up to you if they pass while you're extracting so you can boost them.

His Passive Ability, '''Sugar Rush''', grants everyone (including himself) a +20% Movement Speed boost for 5 seconds at the beginning of a [[Floor]] and at Panic Mode, functioning much like a global {{TI|Pull Toy|name=true}} and {{TI|Alarm|name=true}}. This is very useful, especially for slow Toons who have trouble reaching the elevator, since a +20% Movement Speed boost is decently potent. It also can help you (or your distractor, depending on how you wish to play Gourdy) find Twisteds faster at the start of a Floor and conserve a bit of Stamina at Panic Mode, and also help with reaching [[Machines]] faster.

Gourdy also has great distracting capabilities. He has 1 Star Stealth, meaning he can passively grab aggro from any Toon (besides {{CI|Brightney|name=true}}, who he may have to get closer to grab aggro from, and {{CI|Shrimpo|name=true}}), which is extremely helpful for distracting. Unlike Brightney, he has 4 Star Movement Speed, giving him a better distraction playstyle. His Stamina is much lower than hers in comparison though, so be careful and only sprint/sprint-tap when needed.

Given Gourdy's extreme general potential, there is no specific recommended playstyle for him. As such, given his relatively easy requirements compared to other Mains, he is a very good pick for anyone experienced with using [[Main Characters]]. There is no "wrong" way to play Gourdy, as his only three deficits (Stealth, Health, and Stamina) can mostly be mitigated by strategy, so feel free to give yourself leeway in trying different [[Trinket]] combinations, since there really isn't much punishment gameplay-wise if you lean into a specific role.

Overall, Gourdy is great in teams and selflessness with great extracting, distracting, and supporting potential, with a bit of a struggle in solo runs, though he can still be a good pick as you can easily make his Ability self-sufficient with a Trinket like {{TI|Peppermint Icing|name=true}} and {{TI|Ghost Snakes In A Can|name=true}}.

